# FileBrowser 1.2 - a ListBrowser with an icon view

FileBrowser is a subclass of listbrowser.gadget that can also show its
list as icons: each node becomes a cell with its image or icon on top and
its column texts below, laid out in a grid like a Workbench drawer window.

This is a developer release. FileBrowser is not a library: you add
`FileBrowser.c` to your program's build and include `FileBrowser.h`.

FileBrowser is an [OpenAmiga.org project](http://openamiga.org/?function=gitproject&name=FileBrowser).
The source code, test versions and bug reports are at
<https://github.com/OpenAmiga-org/FileBrowser>.

In the default details mode the gadget *is* a ListBrowser: every method
goes to listbrowser.gadget, so you can replace a ListBrowser with a
FileBrowser and nothing changes. One attribute switches to the icon view:

    SetGadgetAttrs(gad, win, NULL, FILEBROWSER_ViewMode, FBVM_ICONS, TAG_DONE);

The list, the column info and your event handling stay as they are:
selection, double-clicks (`LBRE_DOUBLECLICK`), `LISTBROWSER_Selected` and
the other selection attributes keep working in both views.

## Icon view

- Cells flow left to right, top to bottom; the view scrolls vertically.
- The node's `LBNCA_Image`, or a full size icon (struct DiskObject) that
  your program returns from `FILEBROWSER_IconHook`.
- `FILEBROWSER_IconSize` gives every cell the same image box, like an
  icon grid: larger icons are scaled down, keeping their aspect ratio.
- One text line per list column; `FILEBROWSER_HiddenColumns` hides some.
- Click, Shift-click, double-click, a selection box (lasso) dragged from
  empty space, the mouse wheel, and dragging selected cells for your own
  drag & drop.
- Drawn through an offscreen bitmap, so scrolling doesn't flicker.

## Contents

- `FileBrowser/FileBrowser.readme`: this file
- `FileBrowser/LICENSE`: public domain (The Unlicense)
- `FileBrowser/Autodocs/FileBrowser.doc`: the class: attributes, methods,
  functions
- `FileBrowser/Include/FileBrowser.h`: the attributes and
  `MakeFileBrowserClass()`
- `FileBrowser/Source/FileBrowser.c`: the class
- `FileBrowser/Demo/FileBrowserDemo`: a ready-built example: lists a
  drawer (`SYS:` by default, or `FileBrowserDemo <drawer>`) and a button
  switches between the two views
- `FileBrowser/Demo/FileBrowserDemo.c`, `Demo/makefile`: its source;
  `make` in `Demo/` builds it

## Using it

1. Copy `Include/FileBrowser.h` and `Source/FileBrowser.c` into your
   project and compile `FileBrowser.c` with the rest of your program.
2. Open the interfaces the class uses (it uses your program's global
   interface pointers): `IExec`, `IIntuition`, `IGraphics`, `ILayers`,
   `IUtility`, `IIcon` and `IListBrowser`.
3. Make the class, then gadgets from it (example below).
4. When the windows are gone: `FreeFileBrowserClass(cl)`.

Example for step 3:

    #include "FileBrowser.h"

    struct IClass *cl = MakeFileBrowserClass();

    Object *gad = NewObject(cl, NULL,
        GA_ID,                    GID_FILES,
        GA_RelVerify,             TRUE,
        LISTBROWSER_Labels,       &list,
        LISTBROWSER_ColumnInfo,   columninfo,
        LISTBROWSER_ColumnTitles, TRUE,
        LISTBROWSER_MultiSelect,  TRUE,
        TAG_DONE);

The class is private: objects are made with the class pointer, not by
name. `Autodocs/FileBrowser.doc` describes every attribute; `Demo/` shows
a complete program.

Building the demo: the makefile uses `ppc-amigaos-gcc`, as in the
AmigaOS 4 SDK and in cross compilers. If your SDK only has `gcc`, run
`make CC=gcc`. `FileBrowser.c` doesn't depend on a C library: it only
uses AmigaOS interfaces, so it builds with newlib (the default) as well
as clib4. The demo is written for newlib: it defines `IUtility` itself,
which clib4's startup code also does.

`FILEBROWSER_VERSION` and `FILEBROWSER_REVISION` in `FileBrowser.h` tell
which release a copy of the header belongs to.

